    "status": "SUCCESS",
    "response": "It is completely understandable that this situation feels frustrating and suspicious. On paper, your qualifications—a completed PhD, six peer-reviewed publications, and four years of teaching experience—appear substantially stronger in traditional academic metrics than those of a candidate who has not yet finished their doctorate and has fewer publications.\n\nHere is an objective breakdown of what the composition of the panel might mean, how academic hiring decisions often work in practice, and what steps you can take next.\n\n---\n\n### 1. Is an all-male panel a sign of anything?\n\n#### **A. Institutional Norms and Procedural Lapses**\n* **University Policy:** Many public universities have explicit policies requiring gender diversity on search and selection committees (often mandating at least one woman, or balanced representation). An all-male panel could be a violation of the institution's own recruitment guidelines or code of practice.\n* **Underrepresentation in Economics:** Economics departments notoriously suffer from a significant gender imbalance, particularly at senior ranks (Full and Associate Professor). While an all-male panel may simply reflect the senior demographics of that specific department, best practice at modern universities is usually to co-opt an external woman from a related faculty (e.g., Business, Public Policy, or Statistics) to ensure balance.\n\n#### **B. Risk of Bias and Homophily**\n* An all-male panel does not, on its own, prove illegal discrimination, but it substantially increases the likelihood of **unconscious bias** and **homophily** (\"affinity bias\"). \n* Extensive academic literature (particularly within economics) has documented that male-dominated committees frequently evaluate male candidates differently—often judging men on perceived \"potential\" or intangible qualities, while judging women strictly on past performance and credentials.\n* The emphasis placed on \"strong industry experience\" could be a post-hoc rationalization to justify appointing a preferred candidate whose traditional academic metrics were weaker.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Alternative, Non-Discriminatory Explanations\n\nTo assess the situation objectively, it is also worth considering legitimate departmental priorities that might have influenced the decision:\n* **Strategic Role Fit:** The department may have designed the role specifically to connect students with corporate or public-sector careers, teach specialized applied courses, or build an industry-facing master's program.\n* **Subfield Specialization:** If the other candidate works in a specific niche that the department urgently needed to fill (e.g., econometrics, financial engineering, or machine learning applications in economics) and that matched the position's discreet curriculum needs.\n* **Interview/Job Talk Dynamics:** Sometimes a hiring panel places disproportionate weight on the 45-minute job talk or interview chemistry over the long-term CV track record.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Why Have You Not Received Feedback?\n\nA two-week silence after requesting feedback is relatively common, for several reasons:\n* **HR Bureaucracy:** Selection panels are usually instructed **not** to provide informal feedback directly. Notes and scoring sheets typically have to be consolidated and vetted by University Human Resources.\n* **Caution Around Liability:** If the committee or HR recognizes that the appointment deviated from standard profile expectations or diversity norms, they may be exercising extra caution in how they draft the response.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Recommended Next Steps\n\nIf you want to look into this further without unnecessarily jeopardizing your professional standing, consider the following approach:\n\n1. **Review the University's Recruitment Policy:**\n   * Public universities generally make their staff recruitment policies, equal opportunity guidelines, and search committee regulations public or easily accessible. Check whether they mandate gender representation on selection panels.\n2. **Follow Up Through Official HR Channels:**\n   * Send a polite, professional follow-up to the HR contact/recruitment coordinator (rather than the committee members directly):\n     > *\"I am writing to follow up on my request sent on [Date] regarding constructive feedback on my interview and application for the position of Lecturer in Economics. As an active researcher and educator, I value feedback from the panel to inform my future academic applications.\"*\n3. **Check Public Records / Transparency Rights:**\n   * Because it is a public university, candidates in many jurisdictions have the right under public records laws, Freedom of Information (FOI), or Data Protection/Subject Access legislation to request the interview scoring matrix and notes associated with their own evaluation.\n4. **Consult a Union or Academic Association:**\n   * If you are a member of an academic union, professional association (e.g., Royal Economic Society, American Economic Association's Committee on the Status of Women in the Economics Profession [CSWEP], or national equivalent), or if the university has a staff ombudsman, you can often seek confidential advice on whether procedural irregularities occurred.\n5. **Weigh the Strategic Costs:**\n   * Academic circles in economics can be small and interconnected. If you decide to raise questions about panel composition, it is usually most effective to frame the inquiry around **procedural adherence to university recruitment policy**, rather than directly asserting discrimination before reviewing their formal feedback or scoring rubrics.\n",
